+ARG BASE_IMAGE
+FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}
+ARG RUNNER_BUILD_ID=runner-frontend
+LABEL org.tensorfold.runner.frontend.build=${RUNNER_BUILD_ID}
+RUN nice -n 19 sh -ec 'apt-get update; DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends clang-18 lld-18; ln -sf clang-18 /usr/bin/clang; ln -sf ld.lld-18 /usr/bin/ld.lld; apt-get clean; find /var/lib/apt/lists -type f -delete'
+COPY tf-runner-frontend-check /opt/tensorfold/bin/tf-runner-frontend-check
+COPY runner-entrypoint /opt/tensorfold/bin/runner-entrypoint
+RUN nice -n 19 /opt/tensorfold/bin/tf-runner-frontend-check --require
+ARG FRONTEND_TREE
+LABEL org.tensorfold.runner.frontend.tree=${FRONTEND_TREE}
+ENTRYPOINT ["/opt/tensorfold/bin/runner-entrypoint"]
diff --git a/packaging/runner/README.md b/packaging/runner/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..eb466a700
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packaging/runner/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# Circuit frontend image layer
+
+Build `zig build runner-frontend-check` with the repository's pinned Zig. Then run:
+
+```sh
+packaging/runner/build.sh BASE_IMAGE TAG zig-out/bin/tf-runner-frontend-check
+packaging/runner/check-image.sh TAG
+```
+
+Use a derived serving base that already contains the engine and its lane Python layer. The layer adds Ubuntu 24.04's clang-18 and lld-18, with `/usr/bin/clang` and `/usr/bin/ld.lld` aliases. No cross libc, RISC-V emulator or Python dependency is added for compilation. Host Python is used only to prepare the build context and report image sizes. The builder preserves the base image's entrypoint arguments and CMD; it does not replace engine binaries. Source provenance can be set with `TF_RUNNER_FRONTEND_TREE`; `TF_RUNNER_BUILD_ID` labels task-owned build containers for the shared-node pause guard.
+
+At every container boot, `tf-runner-frontend-check` compiles a tiny freestanding C program through the production frontend and validates its RV32IM+Zba ELF. It never runs that ELF on the host. The Docker build runs the same check with `--require` so an image without a functioning RISC-V toolchain is rejected.
+
+An image that ships a circuit also proves the route the compiled programs take. `tf-runner-frontend-check --require --circuit PATH` compiles the cells in `zig/src/core/runner_probe.zig` (Python loops with `*`, `//` and `%`, and a C string read from ROM bytes) and runs them on that circuit; any output but the expected one, or a run past 100,000 steps, exits 1. `check-image.sh` runs it on `/opt/tensorfold/share/runner/calc.tfpc` when the image has one, and a serving image's Dockerfile should run it as a `RUN` step after copying its circuit. The engine runs the same probe when it opens the runner (`TF_DSV41_RUNNER=1`) and refuses to start on a wrong output (about a second at boot).
+
+At runtime, a missing or broken compiler/linker reports unavailable and allows ordinary boot when `TF_DSV41_RUNNER` is unset, empty, 0, false, off or no. Any other runner value requires a successful check and fails boot otherwise. `TF_RUNNER_CLANG` and `TF_RUNNER_LLD` override compiler/linker paths for deployment and diagnostics. The production frontend also independently abstains if compilation is unavailable; boot does not turn a missing optional dependency into model output.
+
+Runner threads (each cell's translation, which also starts clang and ld.lld, and each circuit run) leave the engine thread's CPU pin when they start. `TF_DSV41_RUNNER_CPUS` sets where they run: `auto` (default) takes the container's cpuset, removes the CPUs the engine pins (the TP plan link's, `TF_TP_PLAN_PIN`, and the RoCE proxy's, `TF_TP_ROCE_CPU`) and, when core sizes differ (smallest capacity under 80% of the largest), keeps the small cores; on a GB10 rank with the plan link on CPU 19 that is 0-4,10-14. `inherit` keeps the previous behaviour (the engine thread's CPU); a list such as `0-4,10-14` is used as given. `TF_DSV41_RUNNER_NICE` (default 10, 0-19) is the least niceness they run at; a higher inherited niceness is kept. The engine logs the resolved set at the first run (`in-model runner threads: CPUs ...`), and the threads are named `tf-runner`. `zig build test-runner-host` checks the placement; `zig build bench-runner-place` measures an engine host-loop stand-in beside the runner at full load (`runner-place-bench ENGINE_CPU SECONDS WORKERS ROUNDS`, `TF_RUNNER_CIRCUIT` for a real circuit).
+
+Exact decimal/fraction arithmetic is separately gated by `TF_DSV41_RUNNER_EXACT` (default off), through the engine's frontend context. Default Python output remains CPython-identical: unsupported floating, decimal and rational output abstains. Installing the toolchain does not enable either runner knob.
+
+Build on a CPU node at nice 19. On shared nodes, stop the build session and pause its labelled Docker build containers while QUIET exists; stopping only the Docker client does not stop daemon-owned build work. No GPU access is needed. `check-image.sh` uses the runc runtime, no network and no GPU mounts, and checks normal/required boot, missing clang/LLD, successful compiler without ELF, and argument preservation. It never launches the engine.
+
+The builder emits a JSON receipt with tag, immutable image/base IDs, sizes and size delta reported by `docker image inspect .Size`. Docker storage backends may report additional unpacked disk usage in `docker image ls`; record that separately when budgeting local storage. The base engine revision label is inherited; the frontend source tree is recorded separately.
diff --git a/packaging/runner/build.sh b/packaging/runner/build.sh
new file mode 100755
index 000000000..da3f8b340
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packaging/runner/build.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -euo pipefail
+if [[ $# != 3 ]]; then echo 'usage: build.sh BASE_IMAGE TAG BOOT_CHECK_BINARY' >&2; exit 2; fi
+base=$1 tag=$2 binary=$3
+here=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
+context=$(mktemp -d -t tf-runner-image.XXXXXXXX)
+cleanup() { find "$context" -maxdepth 1 -type f -delete; rmdir "$context"; }
+trap cleanup EXIT
+cp "$here/Dockerfile" "$context/Dockerfile"
+cp "$binary" "$context/tf-runner-frontend-check"
+chmod 755 "$context/tf-runner-frontend-check"
+docker image inspect "$base" > "$context/base.json"
+base_id=$(docker image inspect -f '{{.Id}}' "$base")
+python3 - "$context" <<'PY'
+import json, pathlib, shlex, sys
+root=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])
+config=json.loads((root/'base.json').read_text())[0]['Config']
+entry=config.get('Entrypoint') or []
+if '/opt/tensorfold/bin/runner-entrypoint' in entry:
+    raise SystemExit('base already contains runner-entrypoint; use the original serving base')
+script='#!/bin/sh\nset -eu\n/opt/tensorfold/bin/tf-runner-frontend-check\nexec '
+script+=' '.join(shlex.quote(arg) for arg in entry)
+script+=' "$@"\n'
+(root/'runner-entrypoint').write_text(script)
+(root/'runner-entrypoint').chmod(0o755)
+with (root/'Dockerfile').open('a') as f:
+    f.write('CMD '+json.dumps(config.get('Cmd') or [])+'\n')
+PY
+DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 nice -n 19 ionice -c3 docker build \
+    --build-arg "BASE_IMAGE=$base_id" \
+    --build-arg "FRONTEND_TREE=${TF_RUNNER_FRONTEND_TREE:-unknown}" \
+    --build-arg "RUNNER_BUILD_ID=${TF_RUNNER_BUILD_ID:-runner-frontend}" \
+    -t "$tag" "$context" >&2
+docker image inspect "$tag" > "$context/derived.json"
+python3 - "$context" "$tag" <<'PY'
+import json, pathlib, sys
+root=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])
+base=json.loads((root/'base.json').read_text())[0]
+image=json.loads((root/'derived.json').read_text())[0]
+assert (image['Config'].get('Cmd') or []) == (base['Config'].get('Cmd') or [])
+assert image['RootFS']['Layers'][:len(base['RootFS']['Layers'])] == base['RootFS']['Layers']
+print(json.dumps({'size_metric':'docker image inspect .Size','tag':sys.argv[2],'image_id':image['Id'],'base_id':base['Id'],
+ 'base_bytes':base['Size'],'image_bytes':image['Size'],'delta_bytes':image['Size']-base['Size'],
+ 'frontend_tree':image['Config']['Labels']['org.tensorfold.runner.frontend.tree']},sort_keys=True))
+PY
diff --git a/packaging/runner/check-image.sh b/packaging/runner/check-image.sh
new file mode 100755
index 000000000..71dce6d0a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packaging/runner/check-image.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -euo pipefail
+if [[ $# != 1 ]]; then echo 'usage: check-image.sh TAG' >&2; exit 2; fi
+tag=$1
+run() { docker run --rm --runtime=runc --network none -e NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=void -e CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES= "$@"; }
+run "$tag" /bin/true
+run -e TF_RUNNER=1 "$tag" /bin/true
+for dependency in TF_RUNNER_CLANG TF_RUNNER_LLD; do
+ run -e "$dependency=/nonexistent-runner-tool" -e TF_RUNNER=0 "$tag" /bin/true
+ rc=0
+ run -e "$dependency=/nonexistent-runner-tool" -e TF_RUNNER=1 "$tag" /bin/true || rc=$?
+ [[ $rc != 0 ]] || { echo "FAIL: missing $dependency did not fail closed" >&2; exit 1; }
+done
+rc=0
+run -e TF_RUNNER_CLANG=/bin/true -e TF_RUNNER=1 "$tag" /bin/true || rc=$?
+[[ $rc != 0 ]] || { echo 'FAIL: compiler success without an ELF was accepted' >&2; exit 1; }
+run "$tag" /bin/sh -c 'test "$1" = "hello world"' sh 'hello world'
+circuit=/opt/tensorfold/share/runner/calc.tfpc
+probed=no
+if run "$tag" test -f "$circuit"; then
+ run --entrypoint /opt/tensorfold/bin/tf-runner-frontend-check "$tag" --require --circuit "$circuit" ||
+  { echo "FAIL: the image's circuit route (compiled probe cells on $circuit) is wrong" >&2; exit 1; }
+ probed=yes
+fi
+echo "RUNNER_IMAGE_CHECK: compile/ELF validation, optional fallback, required failure, argv preservation, circuit route ($probed) PASS"
diff --git a/tools/runner-arithmetic-bench.zig b/tools/runner-arithmetic-bench.zig
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..1dde5937c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/runner-arithmetic-bench.zig
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+const std = @import("std");
+const model = @import("runner_circuit_model.zig");
+const State = @import("runner_circuit_state.zig").State;
+const eval = @import("runner_circuit_eval.zig");
+const rc = @import("runner_circuit.zig");
+fn now() u64 {
+    var ts: std.c.timespec = undefined;
+    _ = std.c.clock_gettime(.MONOTONIC, &ts);
+    return @as(u64, @intCast(ts.sec)) * std.time.ns_per_s + @as(u64, @intCast(ts.nsec));
+}
+fn cpuNow() u64 {
+    var ts: std.c.timespec = undefined;
+    _ = std.c.clock_gettime(.PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID, &ts);
+    return @as(u64, @intCast(ts.sec)) * std.time.ns_per_s + @as(u64, @intCast(ts.nsec));
+}
+pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
+    const a = init.gpa;
+    const argv = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(init.arena.allocator());
+    const load_start = now();
+    const load_cpu_start = cpuNow();
+    var c = try model.Circuit.load(a, argv[1]);
+    const load_ns = now() - load_start;
+    const load_cpu_ns = cpuNow() - load_cpu_start;
+    defer c.deinit(a);
+    const want = try std.Io.Dir.cwd().readFileAlloc(init.io, argv[2], a, .limited(1000));
+    defer a.free(want);
+    const repeats = if (argv.len > 4) try std.fmt.parseInt(u32, argv[4], 10) else 5;
+    for (0..repeats) |rep| {
+        var machine = if (argv.len > 3 and !std.mem.eql(u8, argv[3], "-"))
+            try rc.Machine.boot(a, &c, .{ .kind = .calc, .bytes = argv[3] })
+        else
+            rc.Machine{ .state = try State.init(a, &c), .next = c.go };
+        defer machine.deinit(a);
+        const s = &machine.state;
+        var out: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty;
+        defer out.deinit(a);
+        var token = c.go;
+        var digest = std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256.init(.{});
+        var encoded: [4]u8 = undefined;
+        std.mem.writeInt(u32, &encoded, token, .little);
+        digest.update(&encoded);
+        var steps: u64 = 0;
+        const start = now();
+        const cpu_start = cpuNow();
+        while (token != c.halt and steps < 100000) {
+            token = try eval.step(s, token);
+            std.mem.writeInt(u32, &encoded, token, .little);
+            digest.update(&encoded);
+            steps += 1;
+            const fam, const pay = c.split(token).?;
+            if (fam == c.byte_family and pay < 256) try out.append(a, @intCast(pay));
+        }
+        const elapsed = now() - start;
+        const cpu_elapsed = cpuNow() - cpu_start;
+        if (token != c.halt or !std.mem.eql(u8, out.items, want)) return error.NativeOutputMismatch;
+        const hash = std.fmt.bytesToHex(digest.finalResult(), .lower);
+        std.debug.print("{{\"repeat\":{d},\"steps\":{d},\"run_ns\":{d},\"cpu_ns\":{d},\"load_ns\":{d},\"load_cpu_ns\":{d},\"batches\":{d},\"nonzeros\":{d},\"trace_sha256\":\"{s}\",\"pass\":true}}\n", .{ rep, steps, elapsed, cpu_elapsed, load_ns, load_cpu_ns, c.batches.len, c.nonzeros(), hash });
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
diff --git a/tools/runner-arithmetic.md b/tools/runner-arithmetic.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b0de7fad9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/runner-arithmetic.md
@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+# Compiled arithmetic experiment
+
+`runner_arithmetic.py` builds an arithmetic circuit, rather than RV32 firmware.
+It uses Qwenputer's pinned MIT-licensed IR, exact SwiGLU primitives, P2 token bus
+and DeltaNet heads. The complete upstream license and credit remain embedded.
+No training or host arithmetic-result execution is involved.
+
+The host parses decimal operands and schedules unsigned `+`, `*`, and literal
+`**` (exponent 0..999), with parentheses and unary plus. Each operand is stored
+as decimal-digit bits in immutable DeltaNet rows. The weights load those bits,
+multiply digits, normalize base-10 carries and add partial rows. Intermediate
+results exceeding 62 digits produce `error\n`. Negative values, subtraction,
+division, fractions, gcd and arbitrary C/ELFs are outside this experimental
+frontend. Reject unsupported input and retain the existing calc/RV32 path.
+The expression limit is 255 characters / 128 AST nodes. Large schedules can
+still create large graphs; this is an explicit prototype, not an enabled default.
+
+`--loop-bound N` separately unrolls the sum of squares for integers 0 through
+N-1, for any N in 0..32. Each induction value is an operand snapshot and every
+square and addition executes in FFNs. This is a bounded loop compiler example;
+it is not an ELF translator. The original C loop ELF and an optimized RV32 ELF
+provide independent output and firmware comparisons.
+
+Operand heads read only at GO. The circuit latches computed digit bits and the
+overflow flag in DeltaNet result rows, then a cursor selects each output byte.
+Later positions feed zero operands to the arithmetic blocks; the existing
+sparse CPU evaluator can skip their inactive units. It still visits the blocks,
+so this is not a claim that GPU arithmetic layers have zero cost while printing.
+Its generated stream is exactly
+`BYTE(stdout[0]), ..., BYTE(stdout[-1]), HALT`, starting from input `GO`.
+Thus a successful D-digit unsigned answer takes D+2 generated steps. Internal
+RV32 trace tokens have deliberately been removed. This preserves output byte
+tokens and HALT, not the old ISA trace. The compiler never embeds answer digits;
+changing an operand's snapshot bit changes the computation without editing FFNs.
+
+## Experimental container and entry point
+
+Files use `.tfac`, magic `TFARITH1`, and JSON format
+`tensorfold.runner.arithmetic`, version 1. The rest of the 64-byte header is
+identical to TFIMR001: little-endian `<8sIIQII32s`, version 1, header size 64,
+payload byte count, zero flags/reserved, then SHA-256 of the UTF-8 JSON payload.
+JSON encoding is deterministic, compact and sorted by object key. All numeric
+weights and snapshot values use the same exact f64 JSON representation as P2;
+token files use little-endian u32. Source, constants, dimensions, features,
+nodes, token families and output code retain the P2 schema and ordering.
+`program.kind` is `compiled_arithmetic`; its expression or loop bound, 62-digit
+limit and reduced trace contract replace ROM/DROM/ISA metadata. Operand rows
+contain A_REF in columns 0/1 and A_BIT times each bit in columns 2 onward, at
+key row 0; one head packs at most 31 decimal digits. Operand queries are gated by
+GO, with tiny=2^-90 defining a zero read at other positions. Result heads write
+only on GO and read row 0 at every position; columns 2..125 pack up to 31 digits
+and column 126 retains the overflow flag. The cursor is a standard Qwenputer
+decay head. No ISA RAM or program image is required.
+
+The entry point is `State.init(snapshot)` followed by `GO`. Do not call the
+existing `Machine.boot(calc|elf)`, patch an expression into RV32 RAM, or replace
+an ELF in these graphs. Production loaders intentionally reject the experimental
+magic. Integration needs an explicit compiled-arithmetic program kind and
+frontend, while retaining the same FFN/DeltaNet evaluation primitives.
+This prototype is not wired into the model or the existing calc asset.
+
+For matched native measurements, `runner_steps_check.py` additionally writes
+`.bench.tfpc` wrappers around the exact same graph and snapshots. Those wrappers
+supply the original baseline's unused ROM/RAM metadata so the unmodified T2
+loader can parse it. They carry an explicit `experimental.entry` warning and
+must be run through `State.init` and `eval.step`, never `Machine.boot`.
+They are disposable benchmark inputs, not deployable firmware circuits.
+
+## Verification
+
+Run only on an authorized node inside its session/QUIET guard, after repository
+fmt, lean identity comparison, combined test/contract/golden/fuzz gates and build.
+For an in-memory experiment followed by export and replay of the actual file:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONHASHSEED=0 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python3 tools/runner_arithmetic_check.py \
+  --qwenputer "$QWENPUTER" --expr '2**100' --export out/power.tfac
+python3 tools/runner_arithmetic_check.py \
+  --qwenputer "$QWENPUTER" --circuit out/power.tfac
+```
+
+The checker runs upstream's own reference machine, compares its oracle and
+stdout, restores the serialized graph, and checks every next token with
+upstream BF16 projections, SwiGLU gates and DeltaNet exactness checks enabled.
+All prefix predictions matching the reference output stream prove free-run
+causal equivalence by induction. An independent T2 f32 free-run benchmark adds
+native evaluator evidence; its output, step count and full token SHA must match.
+The suite also covers the supported portion of the first 20 seed-0 golden
+cases, explicitly lists unsupported cases, exercises carry/zero/overflow, and
+requires an operand-snapshot mutation to fail next-token replay.
+
+The circuit's extra depth and width matter. Report actual matched native
+latencies, graph sizes and steps separately. Step reduction alone does not
+establish CUDA performance, packing into a stock Qwen layer count, or model
+integration correctness. Carry lookahead and common-subexpression sharing
+remain possible follow-ups. The original `calc.tfpc` / `calc.tfimr` is never replaced.
+
+`runner-arithmetic-bench.zig` is the small native proof harness. Stage it beside
+T2's unchanged `runner_circuit{,_model,_load,_state,_eval,_elf,_rom}.zig` and
+`runner_api.zig`, name it `main.zig`, and compile with pinned Zig:
+`zig build-exe main.zig -lc -O ReleaseFast -femit-bin=runner-native`.
+`runner_native_check.py --help` lists the report directory and original baseline
+assets it consumes. The harness runs each main comparison five times, records
+load/run nanoseconds, and hashes every generated token plus GO as LEu32.
+The driver requires every hash and step count to equal the upstream reference.
+It also checks every one of the twenty routed sample cases and the separate
+wider-limb/optimized-loop ELF circuits, with full internal RV32 traces there.
+
+The `program.input_slots` list gives each operand head, row, digit offset,
+digit count, first column, four bits per digit and LSD-first order. This makes
+caching by operator schedule and operand widths possible: retarget the snapshot
+without compiling or loading new FFN weights. The suite changes a 30-digit
+operand bit, first requires the old reference comparison to fail, then retargets
+the expression metadata and requires the new result to pass with identical
+weights. Preparation cost can only be amortized after that template cache and
+explicit input-slot loading are integrated; current tools build per request.
+The new compiler schedules its single-assignment nodes by dependency level to
+expose parallel FFNs. It preserves each node's reduction order and leaves the
+original P2 circuit ordering untouched.
